From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0240] clock: two consumers share the gate's demand, and the last one hands it back

C2-04 closed the loop for exactly one holder: the gate opens XO/4, verifies it
running, and later withdraws the enable it stored. A clock provider that owns a
reference has to survive more than one reference to it, or the second consumer
in a tree has to open a second demand for the same branch and the ledger stops
describing the hardware. C2-05 is that card: the demand the gate verified is
shared, and the release that ends it belongs to a consumer that did not open the
clock.

Three pieces:

  * retractBranch(token, consumer, stage) is the C2-04 withdraw, lifted out of
    disableClocks() because the consumer that turns the branch off is no longer
    the consumer that turned it on. The rows are the same rows (disable-request,
    write-issued, halt, disable-check, disable-done), the bounded 2500x10us halt
    poll is the same poll, and the ledger verdict is the same: release with
    stopped=true, so the demand becomes a confirmation instead of the IOError a
    bare release reports. Only the name printed as consumer, the stage on the
    store row and the token being released are parameters. disableClocks() is
    now the thin wrapper that passes the gate's own token and the legacy names,
    so the C2-04 ring grammar and every audit of it are untouched.

  * shareClocks() is opt-in through sweet,clock-share, which implies both cards
    below it. It reads the CBCR first (phase=share-request, enable/off/value
    printed) and refuses an already halted or no-longer-enabled branch with
    hold=1 and zero writes, then takes two references on the entry the gate
    opened (phase=share-acquire, holders=2 then 3). opened must be 0 on both: an
    opener would mean the demand being shared is not the verified one, and the
    pass says ledger-lost-owner and abandons the retraction rather than closing
    someone else's clock. The three-holder state is printed once, so the row
    that lets the withdraw happen is visible rather than inferred.

  * The release order is the card. The gate hands back its own reference first,
    then the first consumer: both are non-last releases, so both read the CBCR,
    print running=1 and the value they found, and release with stopped=false.
    That read is the whole claim - "still running, nothing stored" - and a
    release that finds the branch halted logs branch-halted and aborts, as does
    any release the ledger calls last while one more holder is expected
    (unexpected-last-holder). The last holder then runs retractBranch with its
    own token and the stage share-disable, and phase=share-done prints the three
    stores the boot made. A shared pass therefore ends in exactly the C2-04
    final ledger state: holders=1, desired=1, pending=0, confirmed=1, with the
    enable back on the branch.

The ADT side is a deferred implication, not a parse-time one: sweet,clock-share
requires both sweet,clock-gate and sweet,clock-disable, and a tree that asks for
the share without them is refused by validateProperties with
clock-share-needs-gate-disable. When several of the three are wrong the share is
reported first, because it is the switch the operator flipped; the card below it
is still named in the same row. The generator mirrors the relation, so an image
cannot be built with the property the kernel would refuse.

The arithmetic stays small because the sharing pass stores nothing: the C2-04
chain costs 49+polls reads, and the share adds one read to head itself and one
per non-last release, i.e. 52+polls reads and three writes. The backend case
pins that number, walks the rows in order, and pins the ledger state the pass
must leave behind, including the fault arms that keep the demand with the holder
that failed.

Nothing here is reachable from an image that does not carry sweet,clock-share in
its device tree, so the zero-write default, the C2-03 measurement and the C2-04
ring are unchanged: the property is additive, exactly removable, and the
combined tree is the gate+disable tree plus one 4-byte property.

